;; The Cookbook envelope, pinned.
;;
;; Cookbook's prose is sealed in the clients, and there are two of them: the
;; browser, on WebCrypto, and `plurama-cli`/`cookbook-tui`, on `javax.crypto`.
;; Two implementations of one envelope is exactly the drift this codebase spends
;; its docstrings warning about, and the answer is not discipline — it is this
;; file. Both suites read it. A divergence is a red test rather than a Recipe
;; nobody can open six months from now.
;;
;;   ClojureScript  cookbook/test/cljs/et/cb/seal_test.cljs   (make test-cljs)
;;   Clojure        plurama-cli/test/cookbook_seal_test.clj   (bb test)
;;
;; plurama-cli reads it out of the cookbook checkout beside it — the same
;; sibling-checkout idiom cookbook's own deps.edn uses for us-vs-them — or from
;; the path in COOKBOOK_SEAL_VECTORS. It fails rather than skips when it cannot
;; find it: a drift control you can silently not run is not one.
;;
;; ## The envelope
;;
;;   enc:v1:<base64(nonce ‖ ciphertext ‖ tag)>
;;
;; AES-256-GCM, a fresh random 96-bit nonce per value, a 128-bit tag, standard
;; base64 with padding. The AAD is the UTF-8 bytes of "<binding>/<column>".
;;
;; ## :binding, and why it is not the table name
;;
;; The AAD binds a value to what its column *means*, and `recipes`,
;; `recipe_history` and `recipe_proposals` all mean the same four things. That is
;; not a convenience: the server **moves these values between those tables,
;; verbatim, with no key** — `archive!` copies the outgoing row into the history
;; on every save, `approve-proposal!` copies a proposal into the row,
;; `merge-content` fills a partial proposal from the row. Bind to the table and a
;; Recipe's own history stops opening after its first save, with no error
;; anywhere to say why. That is not hypothetical; it is what the first end-to-end
;; run did, and it is why this file was regenerated.
;;
;; What the binding refuses is cross-COLUMN moves, and nothing else: a
;; description read as a useful-when, a reason read as a context, a Scope's prose
;; read as a Recipe's. Those are in :tamper and they have to fail. It permits
;; every cross-table move — that is the point of the grouping — and every
;; cross-ROW move too, since ids are assigned on insert and there is nothing to
;; bind to at sealing time.
;;
;; That is enough, and here is why rather than on trust: every column carrying
;; provenance — source, version, has_human_edit — is clear and unauthenticated,
;; so a reader holding the database file can forge a version's authorship by
;; editing the label beside the text. The AAD was never what protected provenance
;; from them. It protects the one move they could otherwise make without touching
;; a label at all.
;;
;; ## What is in here
;;
;;   :binding           table → the name its values are bound under. Both suites
;;                      assert their own inventory against this.
;;   :sealed-columns    table → the columns of it that hold prose. The inventory
;;                      itself, and it is here for the reason :published-surface
;;                      is: it is written down in more than one place. Both
;;                      clients carry it because they seal and unseal — and since
;;                      publish became a one-way unseal, the *server* carries the
;;                      twelve of it that are a Recipe's. Not to seal anything,
;;                      which it has no key for: to walk them looking for the
;;                      prefix when a publish claims the Recipe has been
;;                      unsealed. A server whose list was narrower than a
;;                      client's would let a sealed column ride out onto a public
;;                      page through the column it did not know to look at.
;;                      Three suites assert their own list against this one.
;;   :published-surface the columns a visitor is served, and therefore the ones
;;                      whose being sealed makes publishing wrong. It is here
;;                      rather than in one client because the publish interlock
;;                      is implemented three times — browser, cookbook-tui,
;;                      plurama-cli — and a client that widened its idea of the
;;                      published surface while another did not would leave a
;;                      sealed column reachable through the narrower one. Both
;;                      suites assert their own list against this, the way they
;;                      do for :binding and the inventory.
;;
;;                      Not reason/context and not the history: a visitor is
;;                      served none of them at any ?detail, so their being
;;                      sealed is not what makes publishing wrong.
;;   :key-base64        the key every :vectors entry was sealed under
;;   :other-key-base64  a second key, used only by the wrong-key tamper case
;;   :vectors           nonce + plaintext + the exact ciphertext it must produce.
;;                      Seal with that nonce and you must get that string, byte
;;                      for byte; unseal that string and you must get the
;;                      plaintext back. `:table` and `:column` are what a client
;;                      is handed; `:aad` is what they resolve to.
;;   :blank             values `seal` must hand back untouched — nil is the
;;                      sixth of them and cannot be written in a vector beside
;;                      strings without reading as one, so the suites add it
;;                      themselves. "Never seal blank" is what keeps NULL ("not
;;                      recorded") apart from "" ("recorded, and nothing"), and
;;                      what leaves the server's mandatory-reason check able to
;;                      see a blank reason at all.
;;   :passthrough       values `unseal` must hand back untouched, because they do
;;                      not carry the prefix. Mixed state is legal forever, so a
;;                      near miss — enc:v0:, ENC:V1:, a leading space — is a
;;                      plaintext and not an error.
;;   :unopenable        values `unseal` must hand back untouched even though they
;;                      DO carry the prefix, because nothing behind it opens: no
;;                      payload, base64 that is not base64, a payload too short
;;                      to hold a nonce. Rule 3 is that a client hands back what
;;                      it cannot read, and it is not allowed to throw.
;;
;;                      Handed to `seal` as its `stored`, one of these is subject
;;                      to rule 2 like any other stored value, and rule 2 is about
;;                      the *value* and not its encoding: unchanged echoes, so a
;;                      client writing back the unreadable text it was shown
;;                      writes a no-op rather than enc_new(enc_old(…)); changed
;;                      falls through to a fresh envelope. Both suites assert both
;;                      halves over every value below. (This sentence used to say
;;                      only the second half, which was the rule before the two
;;                      clients were made to agree on the first — the file is the
;;                      specification, so it says what its own tests assert.)
;;
;;                      This class is here because it is the one the two clients
;;                      actually diverged on: `js/atob` throws *synchronously*, so
;;                      the browser's promise chain never installed its catch and
;;                      one such value dropped a whole response, while `bb` handed
;;                      it straight back. Every other section is well-formed
;;                      base64, so the drift control could not see it. Note the
;;                      two decoders disagree about which of these are even
;;                      decodable — "AAA" throws in Java and decodes in JS — which
;;                      is the point: what both must agree on is the answer, not
;;                      the route to it.
;;   :tamper            envelopes that must fail. Each carries the key and the
;;                      AAD to try it under.
;;   :round-trip        plaintexts to seal with a fresh nonce and read back.
;;
;; ## The key in here is a test key
;;
;; Generated for this file and used nowhere else. The real one lives in
;; secrets.yaml and on a sheet of paper, never on fly and never in a repository.
;; If you are regenerating this file, generate a fresh test key with it and do
;; not reach for the real one — the vectors are meant to be public.
;;
;; Regenerating changes every ciphertext in here, which is a deliberate act
;; (a new envelope version, a corrected binding), not a way to make a red test
;; go green.
